\chapter{The \texttt{cli.medvind} module and the \texttt{hr medvind}
         subcommands}%
\label{cli.medvind}

The [[nytid hr medvind]] commands read the hours that TAs have reported
in Medvind, so that we can check them against what the TAs were booked
for.
That check is HR work, next to the contracts and time sheets of
[[nytid hr]], which is why the commands sit under [[hr]]
(\cref{sec:hr-medvind}).
All the work of talking to Medvind is done by the [[medvind]] library
module (\cref{medvind}); this module decides what to fetch and how to
present it.
This module has two commands:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[nytid hr medvind plans]] ] lists the plans---Medvind's
  organisation units---that the user last worked with, since a plan's
  identifier is what [[--plan]] wants.
\item[ [[nytid hr medvind hours]] ] prints the reported times, one row per
  time, selected by TA, work leader, comment, attestation and approval.
\end{description}
A third, [[nytid hr medvind deviations]], compares the reported times
with the bookings; it needs [[hr]]'s bookings and contracts, so it is
defined there (\cref{sec:hr-medvind}).
Both commands here print through [[print_rows]] (\cref{cli.utils.output}): an
aligned table on a terminal, headerless tab-separated rows in a pipe.
The second form is the point of the command: a script that compares
the reports with the bookings reads it with [[cut]], [[awk]] or
Python's [[csv]] module.
\begin{minted}{bash}
nytid hr medvind hours --leader 'Ada Lärare' --no-approved
nytid hr medvind hours --leader 'Ada Lärare' --no-attested
nytid hr medvind hours --ta 'Tolvan' --comment DD1301 \
  | awk -F'\t' '{s+=$5} END {print s}'
\end{minted}

<<[[medvind.py]]>>=
"""The nytid hr medvind command: read reported hours from Medvind."""

import datetime
import logging
import re
import typer
from typing import Annotated, Optional

from nytid.cli.utils.defaults import default, config_hint
from nytid.cli.utils.output import Column, print_rows

<<constants>>

medvindcli = typer.Typer(
    name="medvind",
    help="Read the hours TAs have reported in Medvind (Visma).",
)

<<helper functions>>
<<commands>>

if __name__ == "__main__":
    medvindcli()
@

The Typer instance is called [[medvindcli]], not [[cli]] as in the
other command modules.
The CLI's start-up mounts every module's [[cli]] as a top-level group
(\cref{climodule}), so a [[cli]] here would put the commands at
[[nytid medvind]] as well as under [[hr]].
Without one, the start-up passes this module over, and [[hr]] mounts
[[medvindcli]] itself.

Every invocation of [[nytid]] still imports this module, through
[[hr]], if only to build the help and the tab completion.
The [[medvind]] library pulls in [[weblogin]] and [[lxml]], and
logging in pulls in [[ladok3]].
So we import the library inside the functions that use it; the
commands that never touch Medvind do not pay for it.


\section{Connecting}

Both commands start the same way: log in and get a [[Medvind]] object.
What can go wrong is the user's to fix, not a bug: missing
credentials (which [[ladok3]] reports as a [[RuntimeError]] that
says to run [[ladok login]]), a sign-on that does not complete, or
Medvind refusing a request.
So we print the reason and exit with an error code instead of showing
a traceback.
<<helper functions>>=
def connect():
    """Log into Medvind; exit with an error message if that fails."""
    import requests
    import weblogin
    import nytid.medvind as medvind

    try:
        connection = medvind.Medvind()
        connection.login()
    except (
        RuntimeError,
        weblogin.AuthenticationError,
        medvind.MedvindError,
        requests.RequestException,
    ) as err:
        logging.error(f"Can't log into Medvind: {err}")
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    return connection
@


\section{Listing the plans}

A plan's identifier is opaque---[[01320000F0VQ]] says nothing about the
unit---so the command prints it next to the plan's name, and a user
picks the line they recognise.
<<commands>>=
@medvindcli.command(name="plans")
def plans_cmd():
    """
    Lists the Medvind plans (organisation units) you last worked with,
    most recent first. The first is the default for `hours --plan`.
    """
    medvind = connect()
    print_rows(
        ["Plan", "Name"],
        [(plan["id"], plan.get("text", "")) for plan in medvind.plans()],
    )
@


\section{Printing the reported hours}

The command fetches the times of one plan for a period, selects among
them and prints the selection.
Fetching and selecting are separate steps because Medvind can only
filter by plan and dates; everything else happens on our side, in
[[medvind.select]].
<<commands>>=
@medvindcli.command(name="hours")
def hours_cmd(
    <<options for the period and plan>>
    <<options for selecting times>>
):
    """
    Prints the hours TAs have reported in Medvind, one row per reported
    time: name, date, start, end, hours, type, comment, work leader,
    attestation status (A when attested) and account.

    Piped, the rows are tab-separated without a header, for scripts.
    """
    import nytid.medvind as medvind

    <<determine the period>>
    connection = connect()
    try:
        times = connection.timelist(start, end, plan)
    except (ValueError, medvind.MedvindError) as err:
        logging.error(err)
        raise typer.Exit(1)
    <<select the times the options ask for>>
    print_rows(
        <<the columns of the hours table>>,
        [medvind.record_to_row(time) for time in times],
    )
@

The patterns of [[--ta]], [[--leader]] and [[--comment]] are regular
expressions, and a user may well type one that is not---[[DD1301(]],
say.
That is a mistake in the command line, so it gets the same treatment
as the other errors: a message naming the problem, not a traceback.
<<select the times the options ask for>>=
try:
    times = medvind.select(
        times,
        ta=ta,
        leader=leader,
        comment=comment,
        attested=attested,
        approved=approved,
    )
except re.error as err:
    logging.error(f"Invalid regular expression {err.pattern!r}: {err}")
    raise typer.Exit(1)
@

The terminal table takes its column titles from the library's
[[ROW_COLUMNS]], so the two cannot drift apart.
The hours are right-aligned so that they line up as numbers, and the
comment, the one column of free text, wraps rather than being cut off.
<<the columns of the hours table>>=
[
    Column(
        name.capitalize(),
        justify="right" if name == "hours" else "left",
        fold=name == "comment",
    )
    for name in medvind.ROW_COLUMNS
]
@

\subsection{The period}

TAs report their hours for a month, and the reports are approved in the
first days of the next.
So early in a month, the month we want to check is the one that just
ended; later, it is the current one.
That is the default: up to and including the eighth, the previous
month; from the ninth, the current month.
The eighth is a guess at when the previous month's checking is done,
so it can be changed with the configuration key [[hr.medvind.cutoff_day]].
Both ends can be given explicitly with [[--from]] and [[--to]], each
replacing only its own end of the default period.
<<options for the period and plan>>=
start: Annotated[Optional[datetime.datetime], from_opt] = None,
end: Annotated[Optional[datetime.datetime], to_opt] = None,
<<constants>>=
CUTOFF_DAY_CONFIG = "hr.medvind.cutoff_day"
DEFAULT_CUTOFF_DAY = default(CUTOFF_DAY_CONFIG, 8, cast=int)
PERIOD_HELP = (
    "Defaults to the previous month up to the cutoff day of the month, "
    "and to the current month after it. " + config_hint(CUTOFF_DAY_CONFIG)
)
from_opt = typer.Option(
    "--from",
    help="The first date (YYYY-MM-DD). " + PERIOD_HELP,
    formats=["%Y-%m-%d"],
)
to_opt = typer.Option(
    "--to",
    help="The last date (YYYY-MM-DD). " + PERIOD_HELP,
    formats=["%Y-%m-%d"],
)
<<determine the period>>=
period_start, period_end = default_period(today(), DEFAULT_CUTOFF_DAY)
start = start.date() if start else period_start
end = end.date() if end else period_end
@

The last day of a month is the day before the first of the next.
We reach the next month by adding 31 days to the first of this one,
which always lands in the next month and never beyond it.
<<helper functions>>=
def default_period(today, cutoff_day):
    """Return the first and last date of the month to check by default.

    Up to and including day `cutoff_day` of the month that is the
    previous month, after it the current one.
    """
    first = today.replace(day=1)
    if today.day <= cutoff_day:
        first = (first - datetime.timedelta(days=1)).replace(day=1)
    next_first = (first + datetime.timedelta(days=31)).replace(day=1)
    return first, next_first - datetime.timedelta(days=1)
@

The function takes the date as a parameter, so we can test it on the
cutoff, the day after, and across a new year.
The command gets the date from [[today]], the module's one reading of
the clock, which the command's own tests fix (\cref{cli.medvind.tests}).
<<helper functions>>=
def today():
    """Return today's date; the module's only reading of the clock."""
    return datetime.date.today()
<<test functions>>=
def test_default_period_before_and_after_cutoff():
  assert default_period(datetime.date(2026, 10, 8), 8) == \
    (datetime.date(2026, 9, 1), datetime.date(2026, 9, 30))
  assert default_period(datetime.date(2026, 10, 9), 8) == \
    (datetime.date(2026, 10, 1), datetime.date(2026, 10, 31))

def test_default_period_across_new_year_and_february():
  assert default_period(datetime.date(2027, 1, 3), 8) == \
    (datetime.date(2026, 12, 1), datetime.date(2026, 12, 31))
  assert default_period(datetime.date(2028, 2, 20), 8) == \
    (datetime.date(2028, 2, 1), datetime.date(2028, 2, 29))
@

\subsection{The plan}

A plan is given by its identifier, as [[nytid hr medvind plans]] lists
them; without one we use the plan the user last worked with in
Medvind, which is also what the web interface opens.
<<options for the period and plan>>=
plan: Annotated[Optional[str], plan_opt] = None,
<<constants>>=
plan_opt = typer.Option(
    help="The plan to read, see `nytid hr medvind plans`. "
    "Defaults to the plan you last worked with.",
)
@

\subsection{Selecting times}

The five selections are those of [[medvind.select]]
(\cref{medvind.select}).
The TA can be given by name or by personnummer; Medvind knows no
usernames, so a username would match nothing.
[[--attested]] and [[--no-attested]] are the two halves of one flag,
and leaving both out shows all times, so the flag has three states.
[[--approved]] and [[--no-approved]] work the same way.
Approval comes first and attestation later, so what a work leader wants to see
is [[--no-approved]], while [[--approved --no-attested]] is what is waiting for
attestation.

Combined with [[--leader]], [[--approved]] means reported to that leader and
approved by someone, not approved by them.
[[--leader]] searches the name the TA typed; the approver is only part of the
value in another field, which the selection doesn't read.
We print no column for the approval, since that value holds the approver's
identity and the flag already answers the question.
So the status column doesn't show approval: an approved time that isn't
attested has an empty status or [[G]], and so can a time that isn't approved
(\cref{medvind.select}).
<<options for selecting times>>=
ta: Annotated[Optional[str], ta_opt] = None,
leader: Annotated[Optional[str], leader_opt] = DEFAULT_LEADER,
comment: Annotated[Optional[str], comment_opt] = None,
attested: Annotated[Optional[bool], attested_opt] = None,
approved: Annotated[Optional[bool], approved_opt] = None,
<<constants>>=
ta_opt = typer.Option(
    help="Only times of this TA: a regex for the name, or a "
    "personnummer (YYMMDD-NNNN or YYYYMMDDNNNN).",
)
comment_opt = typer.Option(
    help="Only times whose comment matches this regex, e.g. a course code.",
)
attested_opt = typer.Option(
    "--attested/--no-attested",
    help="Only attested or only unattested times. Default: both.",
)
approved_opt = typer.Option(
    "--approved/--no-approved",
    help="Only times with or only times without a work leader's approval, "
    "whoever gave it; --leader is whom a time was reported to. Default: both.",
)
@

The work leader is the one selection with a configured default.
A teacher mostly asks for the times reported to them, so [[hr.medvind.leader]]
saves typing their name each time.
To see everyone's times despite the key there is [[--leader '']]: the empty
pattern occurs in every text, even the empty one of a time with no work leader,
so it selects every time.

[[nytid hr medvind deviations]] takes the same option under the same key
(\cref{sec:hr-medvind}).
Two copies had already drifted apart, with only that command reading the key, so
there is one definition, which both commands use.
It has to be here because [[hr]] imports this module, not the reverse.
The help text says only what holds for both commands.
What leaving the leader out means for the comparison belongs to [[deviations]]
and stays in its docstring.
<<constants>>=
LEADER_CONFIG = "hr.medvind.leader"
DEFAULT_LEADER = default(LEADER_CONFIG, None)
leader_opt = typer.Option(
    "--leader",
    help="Only times reported to a work leader matching this regex, "
    "normally your own name; an empty pattern means any work leader. "
    + config_hint(LEADER_CONFIG),
)
@


\section{Testing the commands}%
\label{cli.medvind.tests}

The commands' tests run the real library against the local Medvind
server of the library's tests (\cref{medvind.api}), with the recorded
answers.
We import the server and the answers from [[test_medvind]] rather than
repeating them: pytest puts the [[tests]] directory on the module path
when it collects the test files there, so the import works whether the
whole suite runs or only this file.
We replace two things in [[nytid.medvind]]: [[BASE_URL]], to reach the
server, and [[shared_session]], to use a plain [[requests]] session
instead of [[ladok3]]'s, which would need real credentials.
Those are the module's two connections to the outside; everything
between them and the printed rows is the code under test.
<<test [[climedvind.py]]>>=
import datetime
import json
import os
import subprocess
import sys

import pytest
import requests
from typer.testing import CliRunner

import nytid.medvind
from nytid.cli.medvind import *
from test_medvind import local_server, medvind_routes, TIMELIST_ANSWER
from test_medvind import time as medvind_time

<<test fixtures>>
<<test functions>>
@

<<test fixtures>>=
@pytest.fixture
def medvind_server(monkeypatch):
  with local_server(medvind_routes()) as (url, received):
    monkeypatch.setattr(nytid.medvind, "BASE_URL", url)
    monkeypatch.setattr(nytid.medvind, "shared_session", requests.Session)
    yield received

runner = CliRunner()
@

The output of [[hours]] is not a terminal under [[CliRunner]], so it is
the tab-separated form, and the period on the command line must reach
Medvind:
<<test functions>>=
def test_hours_prints_tab_separated_rows(medvind_server):
  result = runner.invoke(medvindcli, ["hours", "--from", "2026-06-01",
                                      "--to", "2026-09-30"])
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output
  lines = result.stdout.splitlines()
  assert len(lines) == len(TIMELIST_ANSWER["timeapprove"])
  assert lines[0].split("\t") == [
    "Tolvan Tolvansson", "2026-06-04", "09:00", "11:45", "2.75",
    "Student(165)", "DD1301 labbpass", "Ada Lärare", "A", "JXX,110210,"]
  assert all(len(line.split("\t")) == 10 for line in lines)
  assert "<LOADFROM>2026-06-01</LOADFROM>" in medvind_server[-1]["body"]
  assert "<LOADUNTIL>2026-09-30</LOADUNTIL>" in medvind_server[-1]["body"]
@

The options must reach the selection---a personnummer for the TA, and
the three states of [[--attested]]:
<<test functions>>=
def dates(*args):
  """Return the dates of the times `hours` prints with the options `args`."""
  result = runner.invoke(medvindcli, ["hours", *args])
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output
  return [line.split("\t")[1] for line in result.stdout.splitlines()]

def test_hours_selects_times(medvind_server):
  assert dates("--ta", "121212-1212") == ["2026-06-04", "2026-08-25"]
  assert dates("--no-attested") == ["2026-09-01"]
  assert dates("--attested", "--comment", "dd1301") == ["2026-06-04"]
  assert len(dates()) == 4
@

The work leader selects too, and an empty pattern must take every time,
also the two recorded without a work leader, or it could not override a
configured leader:
<<test functions>>=
def test_hours_selects_by_leader(medvind_server):
  assert dates("--leader", "Ada") == ["2026-06-04", "2026-08-25"]
  assert dates("--leader", "Grace") == []
  assert len(dates("--leader", "")) == 4
@

The recorded times cannot tell approval from attestation
(\cref{medvind.select}), so here the local server answers with the four
combinations of the two.
It still goes through the real request and the real selection, which is what
shows that each flag reaches the right criterion, in the right direction.
The approval of the second time also names a colleague, as the recorded values
name the approver: [[--leader]] looks at whom a time was reported to, not at who
approved it.
<<test functions>>=
def test_hours_approved_is_not_attested(medvind_server, monkeypatch):
  def reported(datum, **fields):
    return medvind_time("Tolvan", "Tolvansson", "121212-1212", datum,
                        "08:00", "10:00", **fields)
  approval = {"arbledare_godk": "2026-10-07 12:35:45.000 Grace Kollega"}
  monkeypatch.setitem(TIMELIST_ANSWER, "timeapprove", [
    reported("2026-09-01"),
    reported("2026-09-02", arbledare_orsak="Ada Lärare", **approval),
    reported("2026-09-03", atteststatus="A"),
    reported("2026-09-04", **approval, atteststatus="A")])

  assert dates("--approved") == ["2026-09-02", "2026-09-04"]
  assert dates("--no-approved") == ["2026-09-01", "2026-09-03"]
  assert dates("--attested") == ["2026-09-03", "2026-09-04"]
  assert dates("--no-attested") == ["2026-09-01", "2026-09-02"]
  assert dates("--approved", "--no-attested") == ["2026-09-02"]
  assert dates("--no-approved", "--attested") == ["2026-09-03"]
  assert len(dates()) == 4
  assert dates("--leader", "Ada", "--approved") == ["2026-09-02"]
  assert dates("--leader", "Grace", "--approved") == []
@

The configured leader is a default bound when the module is imported, so a
test in this process, which has imported it already, cannot show that it is
read.
We therefore start a new process with a real configuration file in a home of
its own, as for the renamed keys (\cref{cli.utils.defaults.renamed}), and read
what each command's help gives as the default.
[[deviations]] is here too, although [[hr]] defines it, since sharing the key
is the point.
The terminal is made wide because [[rich]] would otherwise wrap the default in
the middle.
<<test functions>>=
@pytest.mark.parametrize("command", ["hours", "deviations"])
def test_help_shows_the_configured_leader_as_default(tmp_path, command):
  (tmp_path / "config.json").write_text(json.dumps(
    {"hr": {"medvind": {"leader": "Zyxwv Kollega"}}}))
  env = {**os.environ, "NYTID_HOME": str(tmp_path),
         "COLUMNS": "400", "TERMINAL_WIDTH": "400"}
  env.pop("TMUX", None)
  env.pop("TMUX_PANE", None)
  result = subprocess.run(
    [sys.executable, "-c", "from nytid.cli import cli; cli()",
     "hr", "medvind", command, "--help"],
    env=env, capture_output=True, text=True,
  )
  assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
  assert "[default: Zyxwv Kollega]" in result.stdout
  assert "[config: hr.medvind.leader]" in result.stdout
@

The help shows only that the key is read.
That the key really narrows the selection, and that [[--leader '']] really
overrides it, takes a run of each command with the key set.
So a new process logs in to a local server run by this one.
It replaces the same two connections as our other tests, and for [[deviations]]
also what the bookings say, as the tests in [[hr]] do
(\cref{sec:hr-medvind}), since those would need real sign-up sheets.
Under Ada Lärare, Tolvan's times match the bookings and Tolva's count for
nothing; for any work leader, Tolva has reported all her $5.75$ hours.
<<test functions>>=
RUN_WITH_LOCAL_MEDVIND = """
import sys
import requests
import nytid.medvind
from nytid.cli import cli, hr
nytid.medvind.BASE_URL = sys.argv[1]
nytid.medvind.shared_session = requests.Session
hr.coursescli.get_courses_configs = lambda *a, **k: {("DD1301", "mine"): None}
hr.booked_hourly_hours = lambda *a: [
  ("tolvan", "121212-1212", "Tolvan Tolvansson", 6.5),
  ("tolva", "121212-2223", "Tolva Tolvansson", 2)]
sys.argv = ["nytid", "hr", "medvind", *sys.argv[2:]]
cli()
"""

def test_configured_leader_selects_and_empty_pattern_overrides(tmp_path):
  (tmp_path / "config.json").write_text(json.dumps(
    {"hr": {"medvind": {"leader": "Ada"}}}))
  env = {**os.environ, "NYTID_HOME": str(tmp_path)}
  env.pop("TMUX", None)
  env.pop("TMUX_PANE", None)
  period = ["--from", "2026-06-01", "--to", "2026-09-30"]

  with local_server(medvind_routes()) as (url, _):
    def rows(*args):
      result = subprocess.run(
        [sys.executable, "-c", RUN_WITH_LOCAL_MEDVIND, url, *args, *period],
        env=env, capture_output=True, text=True,
      )
      assert result.returncode == 0, result.stderr
      return [line.split("\t") for line in result.stdout.splitlines()]

    assert [row[1] for row in rows("hours")] == ["2026-06-04", "2026-08-25"]
    assert len(rows("hours", "--leader", "")) == 4
    assert [row[1] for row in rows("hours", "--leader", "Nobody")] == []

    def reported(*args):
      return {row[0]: row[3] for row in rows("deviations", "DD1301", *args)}

    assert reported() == {"tolva": "0"}
    assert reported("--leader", "") == {"tolva": "5.75"}
@

Without [[--from]] and [[--to]] the default period is used---for a
fixed date and cutoff, since the configured cutoff is the user's---and
an unknown plan or an invalid regular expression is an error message,
not a traceback:
<<test functions>>=
def test_hours_defaults_to_the_default_period(medvind_server, monkeypatch):
  monkeypatch.setattr("nytid.cli.medvind.today",
                      lambda: datetime.date(2026, 10, 22))
  monkeypatch.setattr("nytid.cli.medvind.DEFAULT_CUTOFF_DAY", 8)
  result = runner.invoke(medvindcli, ["hours"])
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output
  assert "<LOADFROM>2026-10-01</LOADFROM>" in medvind_server[-1]["body"]
  assert "<LOADUNTIL>2026-10-31</LOADUNTIL>" in medvind_server[-1]["body"]

def test_hours_unknown_plan_is_an_error(medvind_server, caplog):
  result = runner.invoke(medvindcli, ["hours", "--plan", "nonexistent"])
  assert result.exit_code == 1
  assert result.stdout == ""
  assert "unknown plan nonexistent" in caplog.text

def test_hours_invalid_regex_is_an_error(medvind_server, caplog):
  result = runner.invoke(medvindcli, ["hours", "--comment", "DD1301("])
  assert result.exit_code == 1
  assert result.exception is None or isinstance(result.exception, SystemExit)
  assert "DD1301(" in caplog.text

def test_plans_lists_id_and_name(medvind_server):
  result = runner.invoke(medvindcli, ["plans"])
  assert result.stdout.splitlines() == [
    "0132TESTNODE\tExempelavdelningen Personal (JXX)",
    "0132OTHERNOD\tExempelinstitutionen (JX)"]
@

The tests above invoke [[medvindcli]] directly, so they would pass even
if nothing mounted it.
The last test goes through [[nytid]]'s own top-level app: the commands
must answer at [[nytid hr medvind]] and not at [[nytid medvind]].
<<test functions>>=
def test_commands_are_mounted_under_hr(medvind_server):
  from nytid.cli import cli as nytid_cli
  result = runner.invoke(nytid_cli, ["hr", "medvind", "plans"])
  assert result.exit_code == 0, result.output
  assert result.stdout.startswith("0132TESTNODE\t")
  result = runner.invoke(nytid_cli, ["medvind", "plans"])
  assert result.exit_code == 2
@
